    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1155">

                <pb facs="1177" n="1163" sameAs="frus:frus1944v07/pg1163"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00112A European War 1939/36679:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Guatemala</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Long</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-06-22T18:00:00-06:00">June 22, 1944—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 23—12:25 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>384. Reference previous correspondence on Proclaimed List coffee and
                    expropriation of enemy alien properties.</p>
                <pb facs="1178" n="1164" xml:id="pg1164"/>
                <p>The Chief of Protocol today brought me from the President a draft of a decree
                    which it is expected will be published today or tomorrow expropriating coffee
                    plantations owned by German Nationals on ground of public necessity and
                        utility.<anchor n="68" xml:id="d1155-f1"/> It is not clear from the
                    phraseology of the decree whether all German property will be expropriated or
                    only holdings of persons who are considered to constitute a danger to the
                    security of the Republic and the defense of the hemisphere. The decree provides
                    that indemnification will be based on the valuation declared by the owners for
                    tax purposes and will be held in suspense until after the war. It also provides
                    that the executive will determine in subsequent decrees the individuals and
                    corporations whose properties will be expropriated in favor of the nation.</p>
                <p>There can be little doubt that this development is not unconnected with the
                    political situation which I have reported in separate telegraphic
                    communications.</p>
                <p>Further developments will be reported by cable.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Long</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1155-f1" n="68" xml:id="d1155-f1-content">
                <p>Copy and translation of this Decree, No. 3115, was transmitted to the Department
                    by Ambassador Long in his despatch 1243 of June 23, 1944, not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
